[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The CLOS fabric is currently healthy; I cannot reproduce the outage. End-to-end verification between racks succeeds: pc_0_0 -> pc_0_1 and reverse both show 0% loss and ~1.2 ms RTT, and iperf achieves ~138 Mbit/s. Control-plane is up across the fabric: all BGP sessions on leaves, spines, and the super-spine are Established and exchanging the rack /24s. For example, leaf_router_0_0 installs 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 (spine_router_0_0), and leaf_router_0_1 installs 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6; both spines have the correct next-hops toward each rack. Data-plane routes are programmed and consistent with BGP on all devices.

Mechanism-wise, traffic is not blackholed: there is a best path selected at each leaf, with alternate paths available via the other spine/superspine if needed. The earlier theory that “all traffic is squeezed down one dead path” is not borne out now.

Localization note: all BGP peerings show recent Up/Down ~00:01:35 at the time of inspection, indicating a fabric-wide control-plane reset shortly before my tests, which could explain a transient outage then; however, it has recovered.

Root cause: Not currently reproducible; evidence points to a recent, temporary BGP/session reset rather than a persistent path failure.

Corrective fix: None required now; to harden against single-path outages and distribute load, enable BGP ECMP on the leafs (e.g., maximum-paths 2 and multipath-relax) and investigate why FRR sessions restarted. 
=== END ===